Next, we show \eqref{eq:Uus-expansion}. Observe that
\begin{align*}
    \Phi U_{+} \Phi^\top
    &= \sum_{k=1}^\infty \big(V J J_{+}^{\dagger k} V^{-1}\big)\big(V J J_{+}^{\dagger k} V^{-1}\big)^\top \\
    &= \big(V J J_{+}^{\dagger} V^{-1}\big)\big(V J J_{+}^{\dagger} V^{-1}\big)^\top +  
    \sum_{k=2}^\infty \big(V J J_{+}^{\dagger k} V^{-1}\big)\big(V J J_{+}^{\dagger k} V^{-1}\big)^\top \\
    &= \big(V I_{+} V^{-1}\big)\big(V I_{+} V^{-1}\big)^\top +  
    \sum_{k=2}^\infty \big(V J_{+}^{\dagger (k-1)} V^{-1}\big)\big(V J_{+}^{\dagger (k-1)} V^{-1}\big)^\top  \\
    &= \big(V I_{+} V^{-1}\big)\big(V I_{+} V^{-1}\big)^\top + U_{+}. 
\end{align*}
We claim that there exists a positive constant $\epsilon_1'$ such that for any $v \in \mathbb{R}^{|\mathbb{S}|}$, 
\begin{equation}
    \label{eq:ius-bound-uus}
    v\big(V I_{+} V^{-1}\big)\big(V I_{+} V^{-1}\big)^\top v^\top \geq \epsilon_1' v U_{+} v^\top,
\end{equation}
Without loss of generality, we only need to show \eqref{eq:ius-bound-uus} for $v$ not being the zero vector. By normalization, it suffices to show that the function $v\mapsto v U_{+} v^\top$ is bounded on the set  $\{v\colon \norm{v\big(V I_{+} V^{-1}\big)}_2 = 1\}$.  
Let $\mathcal{V}_{+}$ be the linear subspace spanned by rows of $V$ corresponding to the non-zero entries on $I_{+}$'s diagonal, and let $\mathcal{V}_{-}$ be the linear subspace spanned by rows of $V$ corresponding to the zero entries on $I_{+}$'s diagonal. 
Observe that $\mathbb{R}^{|\mathbb{S}|}$ can be represented as the direct sum $\mathcal{V}_{+} \oplus \mathcal{V}_{-}$, which implies that any $v\in\mathbb{R}^{|\mathbb{S}|}$ can be decomposed as $v = v^\parallel + v^\perp$, where $v^\parallel \in \mathcal{V}_{+}$ and $v^\perp \in \mathcal{V}_{-}$. 
Moreover, $\mathcal{V}_{-}$ is the kernel of $V I_{+} V^{-1}$, and $V I_{+} V^{-1}$ is the identity mapping when restricted to $\mathcal{V}_{+}$. Therefore, if $\normplain{v\big(V I_{+} V^{-1}\big)}_2 = 1$, then $\normplain{v^\parallel}_2 = 1$. 
Observe that $\mathcal{V}_{-}$ is also the kernel of $U_{-}$, so 
\[
    v U_{+} v^\top =  (v^\parallel + v^\perp) U_{+} (v^\parallel+v^\perp)^\top = v^\parallel U_{+} (v^\parallel)^\top.
\]
Because $\{v\in\mathcal{V}_{+} \colon \norm{v}_2 = 1\}$ is a compact set, $v U_{+} v^\top$ is bounded when $\normplain{v\big(V I_{+} V^{-1}\big)}_2 = 1$. 
After getting \eqref{eq:ius-bound-uus}, we have that 
\begin{align*}
